1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:02,240
The Keyboard

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:05,040
Five, four, three, two, one!

3
00:00:05,040 --> 00:00:06,759
Ready or not, here we come!

4
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:14,120
And where is he hiding this time?

5
00:00:14,480 --> 00:00:16,120
Tom Thomas!

6
00:00:18,039 --> 00:00:22,320
Tom Thomas, you didn't forget about your grandma's birthday, did you?

7
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:23,559
No!

8
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:25,560
Oops! I did!

9
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:28,240
We found you this time!

10
00:00:28,239 --> 00:00:31,559
Hey, that's not fair! It was my mom that found me, not you!

11
00:00:31,559 --> 00:00:33,079
Then go and hide again!

12
00:00:33,199 --> 00:00:34,079
Not now!

13
00:00:34,640 --> 00:00:36,399
I have to draw a birthday card for…

14
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:40,320
My grandmother!

15
00:00:43,679 --> 00:00:46,320
So, we need one clean sheet of paper.

16
00:00:46,359 --> 00:00:47,840
When's your grandma's birthday?

17
00:00:47,920 --> 00:00:48,640
Tomorrow.

18
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:50,759
But your card won't get there on time!

19
00:00:52,439 --> 00:00:53,640
Oh, then what can I do?

20
00:00:53,640 --> 00:00:55,119
Come on and use your noggin!

21
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:56,759
Pick up the phone and give her a ring!

22
00:00:56,759 --> 00:00:59,719
Your grandmother will be really happy to hear your voice!

23
00:00:59,839 --> 00:01:01,799
No, we've got a tradition!

24
00:01:01,920 --> 00:01:03,759
We send each other birthday cards.

25
00:01:03,839 --> 00:01:05,400
And what's the Internet for?

26
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:07,920
Why don't you send off an electronic card to her?

27
00:01:08,039 --> 00:01:10,400
Simka, that's genius!

28
00:01:12,200 --> 00:01:14,319
Oh, this one's cool!

29
00:01:14,719 --> 00:01:16,799
Now go ahead and type your message.

30
00:01:23,479 --> 00:01:25,159
The letter D isn't working.

31
00:01:25,159 --> 00:01:28,000
How can I write Dear Grandma without D?

32
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:30,920
Just let her be a plain old grandma without the D!

33
00:01:31,599 --> 00:01:33,759
But the letter G isn't working either.

34
00:01:34,239 --> 00:01:36,759
It looks like we could use a pack-o-mat here.

35
00:01:36,920 --> 00:01:38,719
A pack-o-mat? What for?

36
00:01:38,799 --> 00:01:41,959
To clean off the keyboard's contacts that got all dirty.

37
00:01:41,959 --> 00:01:43,120
What contacts?

38
00:01:46,079 --> 00:01:50,799
A key on a computer keyboard works pretty much the same way as a doorbell does.

39
00:01:50,799 --> 00:01:55,239
When we press on the button of a doorbell, the contacts inside touch,

40
00:01:55,239 --> 00:01:59,039
which lets the electricity flow that makes the bells ring.

41
00:01:59,039 --> 00:02:02,120
And when we press a letter on a computer keyboard,

42
00:02:02,120 --> 00:02:05,840
an electrical current runs from the keyboard to the computer,

43
00:02:05,840 --> 00:02:08,319
and that letter appears on the screen.

44
00:02:08,319 --> 00:02:12,240
But if there's dirt between the contacts that stops them from touching,

45
00:02:12,240 --> 00:02:14,159
then the current can't flow.

46
00:02:26,800 --> 00:02:31,500
Tom Thomas, what did you drop in here that is so sticky?

47
00:02:31,800 --> 00:02:34,000
It's probably the soda I was drinking.

48
00:02:34,300 --> 00:02:37,500
And so, you shared it with the keyboard?

49
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:42,000
Here's the reason why it's not working.

50
00:02:46,800 --> 00:02:48,680
Where did so many crumbs come from?

51
00:02:48,760 --> 00:02:51,160
Uh, they fell off my sandwich.

52
00:03:12,560 --> 00:03:15,920
What in the world is this red stuff down here?

53
00:03:15,919 --> 00:03:18,839
That must be the sauce for my mushroom pizza.

54
00:03:19,159 --> 00:03:20,879
Oh, Nolik!

55
00:03:21,079 --> 00:03:24,119
Well, now it looks like we're gonna be out picking mushrooms!

56
00:03:26,919 --> 00:03:29,799
The Fixies are always ready to help people out.

57
00:03:29,879 --> 00:03:32,879
But there are some people we really don't feel like helping.

58
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:36,000
I remember when I was working as a Fixie back in one house.

59
00:03:36,119 --> 00:03:37,519
It was a disaster!

60
00:03:37,639 --> 00:03:40,959
One day the owner spilled coffee on the remote for the TV.

61
00:03:41,119 --> 00:03:45,119
As I was running to clean the remote, he starts pounding the TV with his fist

62
00:03:45,120 --> 00:03:47,000
because the channels won't change.

63
00:03:47,120 --> 00:03:49,240
So now the TV is broken too.

64
00:03:49,319 --> 00:03:52,560
Well, with no TV he decides to listen to some music,

65
00:03:52,640 --> 00:03:55,680
and he carelessly pulls the music center onto the floor.

66
00:03:55,759 --> 00:03:59,120
So he tries to fix that himself and manages to break it for good.

67
00:03:59,520 --> 00:04:03,000
And then he sits down on top of his telephone and breaks that to bits.

68
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:07,280
Meanwhile, I'm still busy trying to clean the coffee off of the remote.

69
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:09,920
There wasn't a minute of rest with this guy around.

70
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:12,080
In the end I couldn't take it any longer.

71
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:13,319
So I got out of there.

72
00:04:13,319 --> 00:04:15,319
And now I'm here, teaching kids!

73
00:04:19,719 --> 00:04:22,639
Tom Thomas, why are you eating food at your computer?

74
00:04:22,879 --> 00:04:25,839
Yeah, they don't feed you in the kitchen or something?

75
00:04:28,199 --> 00:04:29,360
Now I know it.

76
00:04:29,639 --> 00:04:30,719
It's not allowed.

77
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,159
You said it!

78
00:04:32,399 --> 00:04:33,719
Now write your message.

79
00:04:33,839 --> 00:04:35,839
And write the address on there too.

80
00:04:35,900 --> 00:04:36,900
Uh-huh.

81
00:04:37,079 --> 00:04:40,600
Mom, do you know what the email address for Grandma is?

82
00:04:41,280 --> 00:04:43,960
Grandma doesn't have an email address.

83
00:04:45,879 --> 00:04:49,200
So what? We went ahead and fixed that keyboard for nothing?

84
00:04:49,360 --> 00:04:50,760
I still need it.

85
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:52,280
And my grandma?

86
00:04:53,080 --> 00:04:54,760
I'll give her a ring on the phone.

87
00:04:54,879 --> 00:04:58,680
You said you had a tradition of writing each other cards.

88
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:03,160
And what? Grandma will be happy to hear my voice.

89
00:05:03,240 --> 00:05:06,360
That's some original idea, huh?

